the eu-us trade pact is up for  discussion at the education youth  culture and sport council in Brussels in  May in parallel with fresh negotiations  on the transatlantic trade and  investment partnership or TTIP EU  ministers will be updated on how  cultural goods and services are being  handled these aspects including  audio-visual works have always enjoyed  different treatment in international  trade negotiations in part to help  guarantee Europe's cultural diversity  but the United States remains keen to  put the issue up for negotiation despite  Europe's red lines in the sport format  ministers are set to encourage EU  countries to develop policies and  programs for eliminating gender  stereotypes and promoting gender  equality in sport this includes the  prevention of gender-based violence and  protecting victims of harassment  meanwhile members will be urged to  ensure that only suitable people work in  sport particularly with minors the  financial crisis has placed an  increasingly heavy burden on countries  organizing and hosting major of sports  competitions in Europe so ministers will  also discuss ways to promote the  economic social and environmental  sustainability of major sports events  the glasgow commonwealth games chief  executive officer will be a guest  speaker in the youth segment ministers  from the presidency trio and  Commissioner vassal you will exchange  views with representatives from youth  organisations the council will also  discuss any you work plan aimed at  developing jobs and learning  opportunities for young people as youth  unemployment rates soar in Europe the  plan is set to run for 18 months and  will be evaluated toward the end of next  year
 

